1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,380
An innkeeper with twenty rooms
is closing out a reservation.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:04,500
Good guest.

3
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:08,960
Three nights, tipped housekeeping,
asked about the trail behind the property.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,440
The kind of person
she'd like to see again.

5
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:12,860
So she goes to send a thank you note.

6
00:00:13,270 --> 00:00:16,400
And the email on the
reservation isn't his.

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,120
It's a string of letters and numbers
ending in guest dot booking dot com.

8
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:22,340
A relay.

9
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:26,920
A week or so after checkout,
the relay stops working.

10
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,760
A year later the same guest books the
same room, through the same website.

11
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:36,240
And she pays the site the same
commission she paid the first time.

12
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:39,170
For a guest she already knows by name.

13
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:44,960
There's a service a small inn would pay
for hiding inside that second commission.

14
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:49,620
And there's one piece of arithmetic that
decides whether it's worth your time to

15
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:50,280
sell it.

16
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:53,970
I couldn't find anyone selling
it who'd done that arithmetic.

17
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:59,420
So what did that second stay actually
cost her, and who gets paid to stop it?

18
00:01:00,130 --> 00:01:04,900
This is The Operator Economy, where we
show you how to use AI to build, own, and

19
00:01:04,990 --> 00:01:07,730
operate a sustainable business of one.

20
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:10,140
Today we're building a
direct-booking practice.

21
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:14,530
It helps a small hotel get its returning
guests to book direct, instead of paying a

22
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:16,810
booking site to meet them again.

23
00:01:16,830 --> 00:01:20,560
By the end you'll be able to take four
numbers from a small property and know what

24
00:01:20,570 --> 00:01:24,470
it can honestly pay for the job,
and whether that's worth your time.

25
00:01:24,610 --> 00:01:29,030
Start with how a small inn fills its
rooms, because it's worth being fair to the

26
00:01:29,090 --> 00:01:30,410
system before we pick at it.

27
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:35,320
A traveller opens Booking dot com or
Expedia, types a town and some dates, and

28
00:01:35,390 --> 00:01:35,990
compares.

29
00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:38,170
So the inn lists there.

30
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:42,170
The site puts the property in front of
people the inn could never reach on its own

31
00:01:42,210 --> 00:01:44,610
and takes a commission on every stay.

32
00:01:44,670 --> 00:01:48,390
She rents an audience she couldn't
build, and that's a fair trade.

33
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:50,430
The commission is where it gets layered.

34
00:01:51,190 --> 00:01:55,550
Booking dot com's own partner help says
it's a set percentage of the whole booking,

35
00:01:55,570 --> 00:02:00,130
fees included, charged at checkout, and
that it goes up if the property joins one of

36
00:02:00,170 --> 00:02:01,540
its visibility programmes.

37
00:02:01,890 --> 00:02:03,990
It doesn't publish the base rate.

38
00:02:04,030 --> 00:02:07,680
Trade reporting puts that around
fifteen percent in most European markets.

39
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:12,120
The preferred badge and the visibility
booster add to it, and the Genius programme

40
00:02:12,150 --> 00:02:15,310
is a discount the hotel
funds from its own rate.

41
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:17,060
Then three things changed.

42
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:22,890
Cloudbeds, which sells hotel software,
reports the share of independent hotel

43
00:02:22,990 --> 00:02:27,820
reservations coming through these sites
rose in twenty twenty five, to about sixty

44
00:02:27,910 --> 00:02:29,890
three percent worldwide.

45
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:35,330
Early in twenty twenty six, Booking dot
com changed how Genius placement works, so

46
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:38,550
the minimum discount no longer
buys the visibility it used to.

47
00:02:39,370 --> 00:02:44,350
And in Europe, more than fifteen thousand
hotels have joined a damages claim against

48
00:02:44,390 --> 00:02:46,950
Booking dot com over price parity clauses.

49
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:50,870
That's Europe, not here, but
hotels are acting at scale.

50
00:02:51,710 --> 00:02:52,510
Who feels it?

51
00:02:53,110 --> 00:02:57,350
The owner running twenty to forty rooms
with no marketing hire and a front desk

52
00:02:57,410 --> 00:02:58,440
that's short staffed.

53
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:03,170
About two thirds of the hotels the American
Hotel and Lodging Association surveyed

54
00:03:03,210 --> 00:03:05,010
reported staffing shortages.

55
00:03:05,799 --> 00:03:07,400
Which brings us to the question.

56
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:11,299
The site introduces the guest
and keeps the guest's email.

57
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:16,090
When that guest comes back, who at the
inn is paid to make sure the second booking

58
00:03:16,150 --> 00:03:16,900
comes to the inn?

59
00:03:17,710 --> 00:03:22,010
And if the answer is nobody, can
somebody from outside get paid to do it?

60
00:03:22,190 --> 00:03:23,350
Nobody at the inn is.

61
00:03:23,990 --> 00:03:25,150
And that's not a lapse.

62
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:29,700
The site is paid per introduction,
so it has no reason to hand over the

63
00:03:29,730 --> 00:03:30,510
relationship.

64
00:03:31,130 --> 00:03:35,590
The desk is short staffed, so writing to
last year's guests is the job that never

65
00:03:35,610 --> 00:03:36,210
gets done.

66
00:03:36,930 --> 00:03:41,450
Which leaves the property paying an
introduction fee to meet its own regulars.

67
00:03:41,510 --> 00:03:43,010
That gap is a job.

68
00:03:43,370 --> 00:03:45,010
I think it can be sold from outside.

69
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:49,540
But only for what it recovers,
and what it recovers is a number.

70
00:03:50,390 --> 00:03:53,269
By the end you'll be able
to compute it for any inn.

71
00:03:53,310 --> 00:03:57,430
First, how much of the inn's
business is actually rented.

72
00:03:57,470 --> 00:04:01,890
Cloudbeds, from about ninety million
bookings, puts the share of independent hotel

73
00:04:01,910 --> 00:04:06,470
bookings arriving through the sites at
about sixty three percent, worldwide.

74
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:12,650
Cloudbeds sells the software hotels use to
take bookings direct, so it has a view on

75
00:04:12,690 --> 00:04:14,950
this the way a dentist
has a view on flossing.

76
00:04:15,890 --> 00:04:21,850
For balance, HOTREC, the European hotel
association, studied three thousand hotels

77
00:04:21,870 --> 00:04:26,270
and found direct channels still carried
about half of overnight stays there.

78
00:04:27,090 --> 00:04:32,590
So somewhere between half and two
thirds of a small property's bookings

79
00:04:32,670 --> 00:04:34,490
arrive with a commission attached.

80
00:04:35,130 --> 00:04:40,730
And nobody has published what a thirty
room inn in the United States actually runs.

81
00:04:40,810 --> 00:04:43,370
That's the first thing the audit measures.

82
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:45,630
Same data, one more thing.

83
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:49,330
Bookings through the sites
cancelled at about twenty two percent.

84
00:04:50,090 --> 00:04:52,370
Direct bookings, about eleven.

85
00:04:52,450 --> 00:04:56,090
So the rented booking costs
more and shows up less often.

86
00:04:56,790 --> 00:05:02,020
The inn is renting most of its demand, at
a price, and doesn't know its own number.

87
00:05:02,850 --> 00:05:04,460
Second, what the site keeps.

88
00:05:05,050 --> 00:05:07,090
Old inns had a guest book by the door.

89
00:05:07,590 --> 00:05:12,030
You signed it, and the innkeeper had your
name, where you'd come from, and a reason

90
00:05:12,070 --> 00:05:13,150
to write to you.

91
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:15,630
That book was the inn's.

92
00:05:15,650 --> 00:05:21,130
Booking dot com's own partner help says
it doesn't share private email addresses.

93
00:05:21,150 --> 00:05:26,580
Both sides see an alias ending in guest
dot booking dot com, and partners are asked

94
00:05:26,710 --> 00:05:28,970
to keep the conversation on the platform.

95
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:33,540
The trade press puts the messaging window
at about seven days after checkout, and

96
00:05:33,590 --> 00:05:35,630
Expedia's at about forty five.

97
00:05:36,270 --> 00:05:39,170
So the guest book now
lives at the booking site.

98
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:42,970
The inn served the guest
and doesn't have his email.

99
00:05:43,750 --> 00:05:48,370
Which means the recovery job has a
design constraint before it has a tool.

100
00:05:48,910 --> 00:05:53,230
You can't win a guest back from a relay
address, and under the site's rules you're

101
00:05:53,270 --> 00:05:54,970
not supposed to try.

102
00:05:55,010 --> 00:05:57,990
What the inn needs
first isn't an email tool.

103
00:05:58,390 --> 00:06:03,770
It's a guest book of its own, at the
front desk, that the guest chooses to

104
00:06:03,810 --> 00:06:04,930
sign.

105
00:06:05,070 --> 00:06:06,210
Fair question here.

106
00:06:06,270 --> 00:06:09,820
If the parts to fix this are cheap,
why doesn't the inn just do it?

107
00:06:10,470 --> 00:06:11,370
The parts are cheap.

108
00:06:11,950 --> 00:06:16,730
Google will show a free official site
link beside the site rates for a property

109
00:06:16,770 --> 00:06:19,570
connected through a partner,
and charges nothing for it.

110
00:06:20,190 --> 00:06:24,170
A small property booking engine runs from
around thirty dollars a month to about a

111
00:06:24,210 --> 00:06:25,030
hundred and nine.

112
00:06:25,870 --> 00:06:27,750
The obstacle is the front desk.

113
00:06:28,190 --> 00:06:33,010
At a twenty room inn, the person who'd
set up the free link, check the direct rate,

114
00:06:33,390 --> 00:06:37,830
write the thank you, and read the reservation
report is the same person checking in

115
00:06:37,850 --> 00:06:39,710
the guest and finding a plumber.

116
00:06:40,430 --> 00:06:42,770
So the direct path isn't a system.

117
00:06:43,210 --> 00:06:46,470
It's a list of small jobs,
and the list belongs to nobody.

118
00:06:47,170 --> 00:06:48,840
Which raises the obvious objection.

119
00:06:49,230 --> 00:06:52,570
If this were worth paying for,
somebody would already be selling it.

120
00:06:53,290 --> 00:06:55,070
Somebody is, at two scales.

121
00:06:55,890 --> 00:07:00,690
Independent hotels big enough to justify
it hire outsourced revenue managers,

122
00:07:01,090 --> 00:07:05,250
outside specialists who run pricing and
channel strategy on a monthly retainer.

123
00:07:06,090 --> 00:07:07,840
Almost all of them quote privately.

124
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:14,320
And there are hotel marketing agencies
aimed squarely at independents, inns, and

125
00:07:14,390 --> 00:07:15,090
small resorts.

126
00:07:15,910 --> 00:07:18,750
One publishes its entry price,
fifteen hundred dollars a month,

127
00:07:19,510 --> 00:07:24,410
and lists past guest email and direct
share reporting among the things it does.

128
00:07:25,610 --> 00:07:31,570
And above it all sit the sites themselves,
which by their own filings each ran more

129
00:07:31,610 --> 00:07:36,070
than a hundred billion dollars of bookings
in twenty twenty five, blended across

130
00:07:36,110 --> 00:07:36,970
everything they sell.

131
00:07:37,780 --> 00:07:40,040
That's the floor under this conversation.

132
00:07:40,930 --> 00:07:46,210
So the job is real and it's paid for,
by properties big enough to fund it.

133
00:07:46,390 --> 00:07:49,530
I could not find anyone selling
it to the twenty room inn.

134
00:07:50,590 --> 00:07:54,980
It's that the inn can only pay what the
job recovers, and nobody selling this has

135
00:07:55,010 --> 00:07:57,050
published what that is.

136
00:07:57,060 --> 00:07:58,290
So let's compute it.

137
00:07:58,770 --> 00:07:59,390
Here's the model.

138
00:08:00,030 --> 00:08:05,350
It's illustrative, I'm making the inn up,
and every input is one you'd swap for the

139
00:08:05,370 --> 00:08:06,560
real property's numbers.

140
00:08:07,430 --> 00:08:08,110
Twenty rooms.

141
00:08:08,420 --> 00:08:10,550
A hundred and eighty
dollars a night on average.

142
00:08:11,110 --> 00:08:13,070
Seventy percent full across the year.

143
00:08:13,690 --> 00:08:16,730
That's about nine hundred and twenty
thousand dollars of room revenue.

144
00:08:17,350 --> 00:08:22,210
Say sixty three percent of that comes
through the sites, the Cloudbeds figure.

145
00:08:22,250 --> 00:08:25,670
That's about five hundred and eighty
thousand dollars of rented bookings.

146
00:08:26,290 --> 00:08:30,850
At an all in commission of eighteen to
twenty two percent, my assumption from the

147
00:08:30,890 --> 00:08:35,530
reported bands, the inn is paying the
sites roughly a hundred and five to a hundred

148
00:08:35,570 --> 00:08:37,039
and thirty thousand dollars a year.

149
00:08:37,770 --> 00:08:40,270
That's where the old version
of this argument stops.

150
00:08:40,630 --> 00:08:44,250
It's the wrong number, because the
inn is never going to move all of it.

151
00:08:44,310 --> 00:08:45,730
It needs the sites.

152
00:08:45,770 --> 00:08:48,710
The real question is what
a recovery service can shift.

153
00:08:49,330 --> 00:08:54,200
Say it moves ten points of the mix, from
sixty three percent rented to fifty three.

154
00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:58,440
That's about ninety two thousand
dollars of revenue now booked direct.

155
00:08:59,010 --> 00:09:03,230
Commission saved on that, at eighteen to
twenty two percent, is about sixteen and a

156
00:09:03,270 --> 00:09:05,570
half to twenty thousand dollars a year.

157
00:09:05,630 --> 00:09:09,750
Take off the cost of the direct channel
itself, call it three and a half to five

158
00:09:09,870 --> 00:09:12,410
percent, the low end being
one vendor's own figure.

159
00:09:12,950 --> 00:09:16,000
What's left is roughly twelve to
seventeen thousand dollars a year.

160
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:19,090
A thousand to fourteen
hundred dollars a month.

161
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:20,370
That's the ceiling.

162
00:09:20,410 --> 00:09:24,610
It's the most a twenty room inn can
rationally pay anyone to do this job, and the

163
00:09:24,630 --> 00:09:25,930
retainer has to sit under it.

164
00:09:26,610 --> 00:09:27,420
Two things move it.

165
00:09:28,070 --> 00:09:32,050
A forty room property doubles it, to
roughly two thousand to twenty eight hundred a

166
00:09:32,070 --> 00:09:32,670
month.

167
00:09:32,890 --> 00:09:36,840
A five point shift instead of ten halves
it, to about five to seven hundred.

168
00:09:37,610 --> 00:09:40,710
The ten points is a
hypothesis nobody has measured.

169
00:09:40,730 --> 00:09:45,030
But the ceiling tells you, in four numbers,
whether a property can afford you before

170
00:09:45,070 --> 00:09:46,290
you've spent an hour on it.

171
00:09:47,010 --> 00:09:51,710
Now picture the company that does this
job, before we shrink it to a first sale.

172
00:09:52,370 --> 00:09:54,030
A direct-booking practice.

173
00:09:54,130 --> 00:09:58,990
One person working for a handful of small
properties at once, owning the three jobs

174
00:09:59,030 --> 00:10:01,290
the desk doesn't have time for.

175
00:10:01,330 --> 00:10:03,610
Findable, so the free link is live.

176
00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:06,330
Bookable, so the direct
rate matches the site.

177
00:10:07,070 --> 00:10:12,120
Remembered, which is the guest book, the
thank you, the reason to come back, with

178
00:10:12,150 --> 00:10:14,960
the guest's permission
and inside the sites' rules.

179
00:10:15,590 --> 00:10:17,210
And one number to the owner every month.

180
00:10:17,670 --> 00:10:20,380
Direct share of
bookings, before and after.

181
00:10:21,190 --> 00:10:26,270
At maturity that's six or so properties,
each priced under its own ceiling, and a

182
00:10:26,290 --> 00:10:30,590
practice that learns from its own audits
where in the room count the job starts to

183
00:10:30,650 --> 00:10:31,250
pay.

184
00:10:31,810 --> 00:10:34,370
Nobody has built that and
published what it earns.

185
00:10:35,040 --> 00:10:38,390
That's what makes it an
opportunity and not a category.

186
00:10:39,690 --> 00:10:41,650
So that's the business.

187
00:10:42,270 --> 00:10:45,430
The question now is whether
you're the person to build it.

188
00:10:46,170 --> 00:10:50,190
What this job actually needs isn't
a revenue management credential.

189
00:10:50,210 --> 00:10:53,280
It needs somebody who can write a thank
you an innkeeper would be proud to have

190
00:10:53,310 --> 00:10:57,610
sent, because a recovery message that
reads like a newsletter with a name tag on it

191
00:10:57,690 --> 00:10:59,550
loses the guest faster than silence.

192
00:11:00,370 --> 00:11:03,210
And somebody who can sit across
from an owner and talk about money.

193
00:11:03,333 --> 00:11:07,743
I spent ten years in hospitality,
including Ace Hotel and Standard Hotels.

194
00:11:07,933 --> 00:11:10,573
I know this business from the inside out.

195
00:11:10,853 --> 00:11:13,553
I haven't sold this
particular service, though.

196
00:11:14,023 --> 00:11:17,123
What a thirty room inn will pay for
it is still something I'd have to test.

197
00:11:18,167 --> 00:11:21,967
Start with properties you can drive
to and owners you can sit across from.

198
00:11:22,047 --> 00:11:24,927
If you've worked in hospitality, or
you know an innkeeper, start there.

199
00:11:25,827 --> 00:11:29,187
The advantage is being let into
the back office and the guest book.

200
00:11:29,687 --> 00:11:31,606
That's a relationship, not a tool.

201
00:11:32,467 --> 00:11:35,607
What does the machine do,
then, and what do you do?

202
00:11:36,427 --> 00:11:38,487
The software does the repetitive half.

203
00:11:38,967 --> 00:11:43,527
A language model drafts the thank you,
the review request, the note about the trail

204
00:11:43,567 --> 00:11:44,167
in the fall.

205
00:11:44,747 --> 00:11:47,487
An automation tool schedules
them off the checkout date.

206
00:11:48,887 --> 00:11:52,947
You do the half that decides
whether it's hospitality.

207
00:11:52,957 --> 00:11:54,587
You set the voice.

208
00:11:54,667 --> 00:11:56,927
You decide who gets a
message and who doesn't,

209
00:11:57,627 --> 00:12:02,447
because a guest who signed the book expecting
a thank you did not sign up for a drip

210
00:12:02,527 --> 00:12:03,807
campaign.

211
00:12:03,927 --> 00:12:07,407
That's what makes six
properties a one person job.

212
00:12:07,437 --> 00:12:08,867
The drafting got cheap.

213
00:12:08,927 --> 00:12:09,787
The judgment didn't.

214
00:12:10,567 --> 00:12:11,627
What do you sell first?

215
00:12:12,987 --> 00:12:14,687
Not the retainer.

216
00:12:14,727 --> 00:12:15,327
The audit.

217
00:12:16,427 --> 00:12:20,767
One property on a fixed fee, which in the
model is twelve hundred dollars and about

218
00:12:20,787 --> 00:12:22,767
twelve hours.

219
00:12:22,847 --> 00:12:25,787
You pull the site's reports
and the property system's.

220
00:12:26,807 --> 00:12:29,867
You compute the commission line
and the ceiling on real inputs.

221
00:12:31,027 --> 00:12:33,886
You test the direct path
as a guest, on your phone.

222
00:12:35,147 --> 00:12:38,047
And you check one thing at the front desk.

223
00:12:38,947 --> 00:12:44,107
Does the registration card ask for
the guest's own email, or does the

224
00:12:44,287 --> 00:12:46,467
inn only ever hold the relay?

225
00:12:47,167 --> 00:12:51,467
The audit's done when the owner has a
baseline, the ceiling in their own numbers,

226
00:12:51,607 --> 00:12:52,827
and a written recommendation.

227
00:12:53,847 --> 00:12:54,927
Sometimes that's a retainer.

228
00:12:55,527 --> 00:12:56,807
Sometimes it's audit only.

229
00:12:57,597 --> 00:13:01,887
And sometimes it's this property can't
fund it, because you're on base commission

230
00:13:01,927 --> 00:13:05,867
with a low share through the sites and
the recoverable is a few hundred a month.

231
00:13:06,347 --> 00:13:07,267
That's the product.

232
00:13:08,827 --> 00:13:12,367
It's judgment, and it's why the
owner trusts the second conversation.

233
00:13:13,667 --> 00:13:19,347
When the audit says yes, the retainer
is the three jobs, monthly, at a

